call_details.response:
The current position has \(x\) occupying the bottom of columns 2 and 3. These two adjacent pieces create two possible double-threat sequences on the bottom row:

- \(x\) plays column 1, producing an open-ended three that simultaneously threatens columns 0 and 4;
- \(x\) plays column 4, producing an open-ended three that simultaneously threatens columns 1 and 5.

Any move that leaves both of those pairs of cells empty therefore loses at once. The only two legal replies that occupy a cell belonging to at least one pair (and therefore eliminate both double threats) are columns 1 and 4.

Column 4 is strictly superior:

- it is a central file, whereas column 1 is a wing;
- it places an \(o\) on the same diagonal already occupied by the existing \(o\) at row 4 / column 3, creating a useful four-in-a-row threat that can be developed later;
- it claims the last unoccupied square of the valuable 2-3-4 central block, forcing \(x\) either to answer on a weak wing or to abandon the bottom-row threats altogether.

Thus the unique optimal action is to occupy column 4.

Final Answer: 4

